---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/Diagram.astro';
import DBox from '../../components/DBox.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/+$/, '');
---

<Base
  title="One machine, one operator, no silent failures — Andrew Yang"
  description="How one production box grew into a dual-runtime platform where every config has a single source of truth and every failure mode has a voice."
>
  <p><a href={`${base}/lab/`}>← Lab</a></p>
  <span class="tag">operations</span>
  <h1>One machine, one operator, no silent failures</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    My production box runs LINE bots with real users, a news pipeline, MySQL,
    Postgres, CI runners, and the monitoring that watches all of it. One
    operator, no team, no on-call rotation. The design constraint that shaped
    everything: <strong>any failure that stays silent will stay forever.</strong>
  </p>

  <Diagram
    viewBox="0 0 920 140"
    alt="Three generations left to right: hand-run docker compose, then GitOps-lite with contracts, drift detection and rollback, then a dual-runtime platform converged onto single systems for monitoring, edge and secrets. The first arrow is labelled with the silent divergence between prod and git that forced generation two; the second with the duplication that migration created and convergence removed."
    caption="Same triptych shape as every evolution here: the wall of one generation names the next. The silent prod/git split created the guards; the guards made a two-runtime migration survivable."
  >
    <DBox x={10} y={36} w={230} h={64} title="Gen 1 — hands on the host" sub="compose, SSH, pull —" sub2="prod and git can disagree" />
    <DBox x={335} y={36} w={230} h={64} title="Gen 2 — GitOps-lite" sub="env contract, preflight, verify," sub2="rollback, drift as a metric" />
    <DBox x={660} y={36} w={230} h={64} accent title="Gen 3 — dual runtime" sub="k3s + Argo, converged seams:" sub2="one monitor, one edge, one key" />
    <path class="flow" d="M240,68 L329,68" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
    <text class="lbl" x="284" y="26" text-anchor="middle">silent</text>
    <text class="lbl" x="284" y="120" text-anchor="middle">divergence</text>
    <path class="flow" d="M565,68 L654,68" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
    <text class="lbl" x="609" y="26" text-anchor="middle">duplication,</text>
    <text class="lbl" x="609" y="120" text-anchor="middle">then convergence</text>
  </Diagram>

  <h2>Generation 1 — a directory that is production</h2>
  <p>
    Docker compose, deployed by SSH-ing in and pulling. It worked until the day
    prod and git silently diverged — someone edited compose on the host without
    committing, while a pushed change never got applied. Two sources of truth,
    each partly right, nothing to notice.
  </p>

  <h2>Generation 2 — GitOps-lite: every fact gets one home and a guard</h2>
  <ul class="clean">
    <li>
      <strong>An environment contract:</strong> a machine-readable file declaring
      which env keys must exist and which secrets must match across files —
      because a mismatched webhook secret fails closed, and no alert arrives to
      say the alerting broke.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>CI deploy with preflight, verify, rollback:</strong> deployment
      compares against the last <em>verified</em> state, not git history —
      because after a rollback, those two disagree.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Drift detection as a metric:</strong> an hourly reconciliation
      check exporting commits-behind, dirty-worktree, containers-pending —
      borrowed from Kubernetes' control loop, at a human-scale frequency.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cron jobs that report to Prometheus:</strong> backups, cert
      renewal, firewall checks all write textfile metrics. A backup that failed
      for four days into a log nobody reads is what created this rule.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Generation 3 — dual runtime, then convergence</h2>
  <p>
    Applications moved into k3s under Argo CD; the host kept the base layer.
    Migration created duplicates — two monitoring stacks, two deploy models,
    hand-fed secrets — and the discipline was in converging them one seam at a
    time, each cutover verified before the old side was retired:
  </p>
  <ul class="clean">
    <li>
      <strong>Monitoring:</strong> dual-run with shadow notifications, alert
      parity proven with a synthetic end-to-end test, then the old stack
      retired. The mutual-watch pattern died with it — replaced by a dead-man
      switch: the always-firing Watchdog alert heartbeats to an external
      service, so even "the monitoring died" has a voice that doesn't live on
      the machine.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The edge:</strong> host nginx and certbot handed over to
      ingress-nginx and cert-manager — after every routing quirk (an
      encoded-slash API path, a prefix-stripping proxy, basic-auth) was ported
      and matrix-tested against the new stack while the old one still held the
      ports.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Secrets:</strong> hand-applied cluster secrets became
      SOPS-encrypted manifests the cluster decrypts itself; Argo CD now manages
      its own installation. Bootstrap trust reduced to one key.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>CI runners:</strong> one fleet split into two by privilege — the
      deploy runner keeps host access; build runners for public-ish workloads
      run as a user that cannot read secrets or reach the container socket.
      The label is the security boundary.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The method, extracted</h2>
  <p>
    Three rules did most of the work. <strong>Every fact has one home</strong>,
    and a machine compares reality against it — contracts, drift checks,
    template-rendered configs. <strong>Every failure mode gets a voice</strong> —
    and "the voice itself failed" is also a failure mode, all the way up to the
    external heartbeat. <strong>Every decision is written down with the
    criteria that would reverse it</strong> — including the things deliberately
    not done, so they don't get re-litigated. The TODO list has entries that
    say "evaluated, don't redo" with reasons; that file is as much
    infrastructure as the firewall.
  </p>

  <h2>What I'd tell my past self</h2>
  <p>
    Rehearse the cold boot before reality schedules one for you. The reboot
    drill found nothing broken — but only because three days of convergence
    work had been verified seam by seam. The drill isn't where you find
    problems; it's where you prove you stopped creating them.
  </p>
</Base>
